<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="phytate degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4702" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Phytate (phytic acid) is the most abundant &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol phosphate (hexa&lt;i&gt;kis&lt;/i&gt;phosphate) in plant cells. Phytate serves as a storage form of &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol, phosphate and mineral nutrients for utilization during seed germination and seedling growth. It is deposited within single-membrane storage organelles referred to as protein bodies and is usually present as mixed complexes with minerals (K&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;, Mg&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;, Ca&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;, Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;, Zn&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;) known as phytin |CITS:[11590064],[8744268]|. Up to 80% of the total seed phosphorus is stored in phytin |CITS:[15700415]|. Although phytate is found in large quantities in seeds, it is also present in other plant tissues and organs such as pollen, roots, tuber and turions (for review see |CITS:[14568069]|). The potential of phytate to form very stable complexes with mineral and proteins confers upon this molecule its notorious antinutritional properties. Humans and monogastric animals lack the hydrolytic phytases in their digestive tract. Therefore, the high concentrations of phytate in food grains and its ability to chelate mineral cations compromise mineral absorption. Undigested phytate in animal waste also contributes to eutrophication and environmental phosphorus pollution |CITS:[11590064],[11906835]|. The degradation of phytate is achieved by a group of enzymes called phytases which have been mostly studied in microorganisms and plants. Phytases belong to a special class of phosphomonoesterases and are capable of initiating the stepwise release of phosphate from phytate. Phytases are used to reduce the level of phosphate pollution in areas of intensive animal production. Addition of adequate amounts of microbial phytases in animal feed improves the utilization of phosphate from phytate, allowing for a reduction in inorganic phosphate supplementation in the diets for simple-stomached animals. As a result, the fecal phosphate excretion of these animals may be reduced by up to 50%. Finally, phytases can improve the nutritional value of plant-based foods (especially grains) by increasing the availability of the minerals chelated in phytin. &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Plants:&lt;/b&gt; The degradation of phytate into &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol phosphate intermediates and, finally, &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol, is performed by phytases which catalyze the removal of all the phosphate residues of the phytate molecule in a sequential order. Each &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol intermediate is released from the enzyme and may become a substrate for further hydrolysis |CITS:[12405789]|. To date two main types of phytases have been described: 3-phytases (&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol hexa&lt;i&gt;kis&lt;/i&gt;phosphate 3-phosphorylase, EC 3.1.3.8) and 4-phytases (&lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol hexa&lt;i&gt;kis&lt;/i&gt;phosphate 6-phosphorylase, EC 3.1.3.26), which lead to the production of Ins(1,2,4,5,6)P&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt; and Ins(1,2,3,5,6)P&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt;, respectively, from phytate. The main phytate degradation route elucidated in legumes, cereals, |FRAME:TAX-4932| and |FRAME:TAX-286|, then, goes from the conversion of either of these two compounds to Ins(1,2,5,6)P&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;, Ins(1,2,6)P&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; and Ins(1,2)P&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; |CITS:[12405789]|. A minor route proceeds through the dephosphorylation of Ins(1,2,3,5,6)P&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt; to Ins(1,2,3,6)P&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;. This minor branch results from lesser activities from the same enzymes involved in the catalysis of the reaction of the main branch, rather than that of the activity of a different set of enzymes |CITS:[12405789],[10879469]|. &lt;b&gt;Note on Inositol Phosphates:&lt;/b&gt; Inositol phosphates containing the (1,2,3)-trisphosphate cluster are iron-binding antioxidants |CITS:[9034232],[spiers95]|, and Ins(1,2,3,6)P&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; has a structure that is moderately effective in opening calcium channels |CITS:[9160166],[8141257]|. In addition, Ins(1,2,6)P&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; has anti-inflammatory and antisecretory properties |CITS:[claxon90]|.</Description>
